r (feature) release. To migrate to 2.0, please refer to 【294†link】 in addition to these release notes.\nL389: ### Highlights\nL390:   * Unified CKKS and GL Backend: CKKS and GL now share the same data structures and computation backend. This allows future improvements to benefit both schemes simultaneously. Previously, most optimizations were applied only to CKKS operations.\nL391:   * Compact Representation Only: `compact` is now the default and only supported representation for CKKS, and the `compact` argument of `Engine` has been removed. Support for `compact` in GL is under development and will be available soon.\nL392:   * Async GPU Mode by Default: The `gpu` mode now runs asynchronously, as the `async gpu` mode did, and is the only supported option for NVIDIA GPUs. The `async gpu` mode name has been removed; use `gpu` instead. This change also significantly improves GL performance.\nL393:   * Evaluation Key: A new unified key, the Evaluation Key, can be used for most CKKS operations that require a key, except encryption and decryption. It cannot be used with `rotate_many` yet, and is not available for GL. This greatly simplifies operations such as bootstrapping, which previously required three to four separate keys as arguments.\nL394: ### New Features\nL395:   * Multi-GPU Support: Engines in `gpu` mode with different device IDs can now coexist within the same process. This makes it easier to develop applications that use multiple GPUs.\nL396:   * Overlapping GPU Transfers and Computation: GPU data transfers and computations can now overlap when using two or more separate engines in `gpu` mode. If engine A attempts to access a data structure that engine B is still transferring to the GPU, engine A automatically waits for engine B's data transfer to complete before proceeding. No additional synchronization is required. Finer-grained synchronization will be introduced in the future.\nL397:   * Bootstrap Key Reuse: Bootstrap keys can now be used for `roll`, `roll_many`, and `rotate`, eliminating the need for separate roll or rotation keys and reducing memory requirements. Support for `rotate_many` will be added soon.\nL398:   * More Multiparty Levels: CKKS multiparty operations now support significantly more multiplicative levels for each slot count. The default multiparty engine now has 10 levels instead of 4. The maximum multiparty level is now 23 instead of 29, because configurations with 65,536 slots have been removed.\nL399:   * GL Parallel Mode: GL now supports `parallel` mode, allowing multiple CPU threads to accelerate all operations.\nL400:   * GL Serialization: GL data structures can now be serialized and deserialized, making it easier to develop distributed applications.\nL401:   * GL CPU/GPU Data Transfers: GL now supports `to_cpu` and `to_cuda`, allowing data structures to move between CPU and GPU memory. This is particularly useful for applications where not all data structures can fit in GPU memory simultaneously.\nL402:   * GL Memory Size Properties: GL data structures now provide `nbytes` and `serialized_nbytes` properties. Representative sizes are available in 【296†link】.\nL403:   * GL Public Key: GL now supports public keys, allowing messages to be encrypted without exposing the secret key.\nL404:   * GL Constant Arithmetic: GL now supports addition, subtraction, and multiplication between ciphertexts and constant values.\nL405:   * GL Polynomial Operations: `make_power_basis` and `weighted_sum` are now available for GL.\nL406:   * Public Security Parameters: The library's security parameters are now publicly documented. Please refer to 【17†link】 for details.\nL407: ### Optimizations\nL408:   * Faster Bootstrapping: Bootstrapping is approximately 15% faster.\nL409:   * Optimized Key Switching and Rescaling: Key switching and `rescale` operations have been optimized.\nL410:   * GL Performance Improvements: All GL operations have been rewritten and optimized to the same level as CKKS operations.\nL411:   * Faster Multiparty Operations: Multiparty operations are now up to twice as fast at a given level.\nL412:   * Reduced Key Memory Usage: Memory usage has been reduced by approximately 3% for all keys except secret keys, public keys, relinearization keys, and small bootstrap keys.\nL413:   * Faster Random Number Generation: Random number generation has been optimized. It is used internally during all key generation, encryption, and multiparty decryption operations.\nL414: ### Breaking Changes\nL415: \nL416: This major release introduces several breaking changes. In general, the removed features and configurations offered limited practical value or saw little actual use, while significantly increasing maintenance and testing costs.\nL417: #### Serialization Format\nL418: \nL419:   * New Serialization Format: The serialization format has been completely redesigned to accommodate the many new features and optimizations planned for future releases.\nL420:   * No Backward Compatibility: The new format is not backward-compatible with previous releases. The extensive changes made maintaining backward compatibility impractically complex. Breaking changes of this kind will not be introduced in regular minor (feature) releases.\nL421: #### Engine Construction\nL422:   * Keyword-Only Arguments: All arguments to the `Engine` constructor must now be passed as keyword arguments. This prevents accidentally passing values to the wrong parameters.\nL423:   * Renamed Arguments: `use_bootstrap` and `use_multiparty` have been renamed to `bootstrap` and `multiparty`, respectively. Note that `use_bootstrap=True` created a 10-level bootstrapping engine, while `bootstrap=True` creates a 17-level bootstrapping engine.\nL424:   * Removed Arguments: The `compact`, `slot_count`, `use_bootstrap_to_14_levels`, and `use_bootstrap_to_17_levels` arguments have been removed.\nL425:   * GLEngine Removed: `GLEngine` has been merged into `Engine`. GL engines are now created with `Engine(form=\"matrix\")` or `Engine(shape=...)`, and the GL-specific data types, such as `GLCiphertext`, have been replaced with the data types shared with CKKS.\nL426:   * Default Engine Configuration: Creating an engine without arguments is still supported. The default configuration is a CKKS engine with seven multiplicative levels.\nL427: #### Matrix Multiplication and Sparse Encoding\nL428:   * CKKS Matrix Multiplication Removed: CKKS matrix multiplication is no longer supported. Without sparse encoding, matrix sizes became too large, making both runtime and memory requirements impractical.\nL429:   * Sparse Encoding Removed: Sparse encoding is no longer supported. Although it was introduced to reduce matrix sizes, reducing them too aggressively also decreased throughput.\nL430:   * Future Matrix Multiplication Support: Current research increasingly focuses on efficient packing techniques for CKKS matrix multiplication rather than sparse encoding. Although GL is now available, its required shapes may still be too large for smaller applications. We plan to provide example code or a library feature to make CKKS matrix multiplication easier to implement in the future.\nL431: #### Bootstrapping\nL432:   * 17-Level Bootstrapping Only: Bootstrapping to 17 levels is now the only supported configuration. Among the three regular bootstrap variants available in previous releases, this variant provides the most levels and the lowest error, with minimal performance degradation.\nL433:   * Lossy and Sign Bootstrapping Removed: Lossy bootstrapping is no longer supported because its error was too high for practical use. Sign bootstrapping has also been removed because no compelling use case was identified.\nL434:   * Removed `mod_raise` and Custom Secrets: `mod_raise` and secret keys with custom secret values have been removed. These features were originally intended to support custom bootstrapping operations, which are no longer possible with the modulus configuration used for 17-level bootstrapping.\nL435:   * Large Bootstrap Key Removed: The large bootstrap key is no longer available. GPU implementations have been optimized to the point where larger keys can actually slow down computation, even when they reduce the total number of operations. Support for larger bootstrap keys may be reintroduced in the future using different optimization techniques.\nL436:   * Custom Stage Counts Removed: Specifying bootstrap stage counts is no longer supported. This option was available only for 10-level bootstrapping.\nL437:   * Merge Bootstrap Key Removed: The merge bootstrap key is no longer available. `merge_bootstrap` can be used with the bootstrap key, the roll key and the small bootstrap key, or the Evaluation Key.\nL438: #### Large CKKS Parameters\nL439: \nL440:   * 65,536 Slots Removed: CKKS configurations with 65,536 slots are no longer supported. Their key sizes were too large for practical use, and encoding and decoding errors were excessive at low levels (approximately levels 0–5).\nL441:   * Alternative for Higher Levels: Applications requiring more than the 36 levels provided by the largest CKKS engine can use a bootstrap-enabled engine.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt: Empirical characterization of ICICLE NTT on consumer NVIDIA Blackwell (RTX 5070, sm_120), with a prototype for the digit-reversal bottleneck. · GitHub (https://github.com/pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 297\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP Regist